\subsection{The Differentials of the Inverse Trigonometric Functions}
\label{sec:diff-invers-invers}

Reasoning from pictures, as we've just done, can be very useful. It
can give us strong insights into problems and we encourage you to do
it whenever you can.  \alert{But.} The most we can hope for when
reasoning from pictures is an intuitive feel for the problem. We
always need to confirm our intuition analytically.

\centerline{\alert{Always}.}

The function name \emph{arctangent} literally means ``the angle (arc) whose
tangent is\aside{It can be helpful to say this (rather than
``arctangent'') to yourself whenever you encounter it. If
$y=\arctan(x)$, think to yourself, ``$Y$ is the angle whose tangent is
$x$.''}.'' 
This means that
$
y=\arctan(x)$ exactly when $\tan(y)=x$.
Differentiating this to find $\dx{y}$ gives:
\begin{align*}
  \dx(\tan(y)) &= \dx{x}\\
  \sec^2(y)\dx{y} &= \dx{x}\\
  \dx{y}&= \frac{1}{\sec^2(y)}\dx{x}.
\end{align*}
In fact, this actually \emph{is} the differential of the arctangent
function. However, there are two problems: (1) it does not seem to
match the results of our intutive argument above, and (2) it is not
going to be very useful to us in this form since we have $\dx{y}$ in
terms of $y$ itself. We can address both of these problems by finding
$\dx{y}$ in terms of $x$ and $\dx{x}$.

\begin{wrapfigure}[]{r}{2in}
  \captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
  \centerline{\includegraphics*[height=1.7in,width=2in]{../Figures/arctanMnemonic2}}
  \label{fig:arctanMnemonic2}
\end{wrapfigure}
Remember that $y=\arctan(x)$ is ``the angle whose tangent is $x$'' and
use the diagram at the right.  Since the secant function is equal to
$\dfrac{\text{hypotenuse}}{\text{adjacent}}$, the Pythagorean Theorem
tells us that
$$
\sec(y)= \frac{\sqrt{1+x^2}}{1}=\sqrt{1+x^2},
$$
so that
$$
\dx{y}=\dx{(\arctan(y))}=\frac{1}{\sec^2(y)}\dx{x}=\frac{1}{1+x^2}\dx{x},
$$
or
$$
\dfdx{y}{x}=\frac{1}{1+x^2}
$$
So our intuition was correct. The Witch is indeed the derivative of
the arctangent function.  A similar derivation can be used for the
arccotangent.

