When we graph $\arcsin(x)$ by flipping the graph of $\sin(x)$ across
the line $y=x$ we get the sketch below. Obviously $\arcsin(x)$ is also
a multi-function, not a function.  For the value of $x$ shown in the
sketch the arrows point to a few of the possible values of
$\arcsin(x)$. There are again
infinitely many.\\
\centerline{\includegraphics*[height=2in,width=1.5in]{../Figures/arcsin5}}

Since $\arcsin(x)$ is not a function it can't be the inverse
\emph{function} of $\sin(x)$.  Once again, to \emph{create} an inverse
sine function (which we will call $\inverse\sin(x)$) we will have to
restrict the range of $\inverse\sin(x)$ just like we restricted the
range of $\inverse\tan(x)$.  The question is, what restriction should
we impose? When we were looking to restrict the range of the
$\inverse\tan(x)$ there was ``natural'' choice that was clearly
visible in the graph.  That doesn't seem to be true this time. We'll
have to find some other criterion to base our choice on.

Looking back at Table~\ref{tab:InvTrigDiff1} we see that the
derivative of  $\arctan(x)$ (and thus the derivative of  $\inverse\tan(x)$) is
always positive while the derivative of the $\inverse\cot(x)$ is
always negative. This happened naturally with the tangent and
cotangent, but the derivative of the $\arcsin(x)$ is
$$
\dfdx{(\arcsin(x))}{x}=\frac{\pm1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}.
$$
Since we will have to make some choices to define $\inverse\sin(x)$, we
may as well make convenient choices. 
The ``plus-or-minus'' in this formula is inconvenient. Looking at the
sketch below do you see that if we restrict the range of
$\inverse\sin(x)$ to the interval $[-\pi/2, \pi/2]$ the graph of
$\inverse\sin(x)$ (in blue) will \emph{always} have a positive slope?
So with this restriction in place the derivative of the inverse
sine will be
$$\dfdx{(\inverse\sin(x))}{x}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}$$
and
we won't need to concern ourselves with the ``plus-or-minus'' problem. \\
\centerline{\includegraphics*[height=2in,width=1.5in]{../Figures/arcsin6}}

As before, restricting
the \emph{\underline{range}} of the inverse sine forces us to restrict the
\emph{\underline{domain}} of the sine itself. So $\inverse\sin(x)$
is the inverse of $\sin(x)$ with domain $[-\pi/2,
\pi/2]$. It is \alert{not} the inverse of $\sin(x)$ with domain $\RR$,
because that function does not have an inverse.

