\end{ProficiencyDrill}

Inverting the cosine function has all of the same issues as the other
functions we've looked at so far.  By flipping the graph of the cosine
across the line $y=x$ we can see the graph of the multi-function,
$\arccos(x)$. The cosine with domain $\RR$ is not invertible so
inverting the cosine  consists of selecting an appropriate branch
of $\arccos(x)$ by constraining the the range of $\arccos(x)$
(equivalently, constraining the domain of $\cos(x)$).

