  As a practical matter we will rarely be interested in
  second order differentials like $\dx^2x$. As we indicated in
  section~\ref{sec:more-high-deriv} differentials in general are really
  just a step along the way to finding derivatives (differential
  ratios). Viewed in this light Leibniz' notation is again very helpful
  for if $y=f(x)$ then the second derivative of $y$ is
  $$
  \dfdx{}{x}\left(\dfdx{y}{x}\right) = \dfdxn{y}{x}{2}
  $$
  as before.  
\end{mynotation}

