Let's apply formula(~\ref{eq:CurvatureDef} to a circle to see if this
agrees with Oresme's definition of the curvature of a circle..
Consider the circle of radius $r$ given by
$$
x^2+y^2=r^2.
$$
Differentiating, we obtain
\begin{align*}
  2x\dx{x}+2y\dx{y}&=0\\
  \dfdx{y}{x}&=-\frac{x}{y}\\
  \intertext{and}
  \dx\left(\dfdx{y}{x}\right)&=\frac{-y\dx{x}+x\dx{y}}{y^2}\\
  \intertext{or}
  \dfdxn{y}{x}{2}&=\frac{-y+x\dfdx{y}{x}}{y^2}
\end{align*}


